# Line endings.
#
# The policy lives here rather than in anyone's `core.autocrlf`, because a setting in a
# developer's own git config is not something this repository can rely on. Git for Windows'
# installer sets `core.autocrlf=true` by default, and that default is the only thing that has
# been holding the line so far - on a machine where it is `false` or `input`, a CRLF file
# committed from Windows lands in the index *as CRLF*, and from then on every diff of that file
# is a whole-file diff for everybody else. Measured here rather than assumed:
#
#     core.autocrlf=true   + no attribute   ->  CRLF working file lands in the index as LF
#     core.autocrlf=false  + no attribute   ->  CRLF working file lands in the index as CRLF   <- the hazard
#     core.autocrlf=false  + text=auto      ->  CRLF working file lands in the index as LF
#
# An attribute overrides `core.autocrlf` entirely, so the line below fixes the outcome for every
# machine, clone and worktree, whatever anyone's git happens to be configured to do.
#
# `text=auto` - git decides text from content and leaves anything binary alone, so an image or an
# archive added later needs no rule of its own.
#
# `eol=crlf` - checked out with CRLF, which is what `src/.editorconfig` already asks for with
# `end_of_line = crlf` under `[*]`. These two files must agree: an editor honouring one and git
# honouring the other is how a repository ends up rewriting whole files on alternate saves.
# Changing the house style later means changing both, together, in one commit.
#
# What this does NOT do is silence `warning: LF will be replaced by CRLF`. That warning fires when
# a file on disk has LF and would be checked out as CRLF, and it is worth keeping: the content is
# never at risk - it is LF in the index either way - but the warning is how you find out that some
# tool wrote against the house style. Writing CRLF, or letting an editor that reads `.editorconfig`
# do it, is what makes it stop.
*   text=auto eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are the one exception, and not a stylistic one: `#!/bin/bash\r` is not a valid
# interpreter path, so a CRLF shell script fails to run at all on Linux and in WSL, with a
# "bad interpreter" error that names a line ending nobody can see.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated protobuf output, which a tool writes and nobody authors.
#
# `npm test` in `src/client` runs `pretest` -> `npm run generate` -> fourteen `buf generate`
# invocations, and buf writes LF. Under the CRLF rule above, that leaves seven tracked files
# rewritten in every tree where the mandatory client gate has been run - and the gate is mandatory
# before merging, so every agent meets it in every fresh worktree. The hazard is attribution rather
# than content: a pathspec-limited commit misses them, but a bare `git commit -a` in the shared main
# checkout takes all seven under somebody else's message, and they look like real work in
# `git status` while containing none.
#
# So the ending follows the generator rather than the house style, because these files are output.
# `src/.editorconfig` carries the matching `end_of_line = lf` for the same paths: the two files must
# agree, and exempting a folder in one of them only is how they start contradicting each other.
#
# This does NOT make `git status` a policy check. Status compares normalised content, so a file can
# read clean while its own bytes have drifted - `git ls-files --eol` is the only thing that answers
# that question, and it remains the way to check any of this.
#
# The user ruled this exemption on 2026-09-23, after it was raised as a policy question rather than
# taken as a tidy-up.
src/client/src/generated/**   text eol=lf

# The `.proto` sources that output is generated FROM, for the same reason one step upstream.
#
# `protoc-gen-es` copies a `.proto`'s doc comments into the `*_pb.ts` it writes, byte for byte -
# carriage return included - while writing everything else itself with LF. So under the CRLF rule
# above, every generate run left each output of a commented `.proto` with MIXED endings: measured on
# 2026-09-25, 19 of the 26 `*_pb.ts` in a tree were mixed (`c4_pb.ts`: 25 CRLF lines among 340 LF),
# identically in the main checkout and a worktree, and the exemption above cannot fix that, because
# a file git never checks out - most of `generated/` is ignored - is never converted by git at all.
# Developer 1 found it. The only place the stray CR can be removed is its source.
#
# So `.proto` follows the ending of the output made from it rather than the house style. Every
# tool that reads these files - buf, protoc and Grpc.Tools - reads LF, and none of them writes one.
# `src/.editorconfig` carries the matching `end_of_line = lf` under `[*.proto]`.
#
# **An existing tree does not convert by itself.** Git rewrites a working file only when a checkout
# changes it, and the index content of every `.proto` is identical before and after this rule, so a
# tree that already holds CRLF protos keeps them until they are checked out again - delete them and
# `git checkout -- '*.proto'`. A fresh worktree is LF from the start. A tree whose protos disagree
# with the ones the tracked `*_pb.ts` were generated from shows those modified after a generate run,
# containing no work - a blank doc-comment line gains or loses a trailing space. Check with
# `git ls-files --eol`; for the ignored `*_pb.ts`, add `--others --ignored --exclude-standard`.
#
# The user ruled this on 2026-09-25.
*.proto   text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documents whose *bytes* are the test subject rather than its input.
#
# Each diagram module's round-trip requirement says a document ADP did not change comes back
# byte-identical, and its tests check that by comparing what the writer produced against the file
# on disk. With line-ending conversion in play those tests measure git rather than the writer -
# and a `crlf-line-endings.*` / `lf-line-endings.*` pair becomes the same file, which is the one
# thing that pair exists to disprove. A `no-trailing-newline.*` fixture is equally at the mercy of
# any tool that helpfully adds one.
#
# These are listed by extension, not by directory. That is deliberate, and a directory rule was
# tried first and withdrawn: `.../Tests/Fixtures/** -text` also catches the readmes, the recorded
# Structurizr verdicts and the exported diagrams that live beside the fixtures, none of which are
# byte-compared, and marking those `-text` would freeze CRLF into the index for files where the
# house style should simply apply. The guarantee belongs to the format - a `.dsl` is byte-sensitive
# because of what it is, not because of where it sits.
#
# **Adding a diagram module?** If its documents are byte-compared, add its extension here. That is
# the whole of the ceremony, and this comment is here so the reasoning does not have to be worked
# out a sixth time - mindmap, c4, azure-devops-pipeline, wardley-map and ansible-structure each
# rediscovered it independently.
*.mm    -text
*.dsl   -text
*.tml   -text
*.dgr   -text
# `.fdg` is the functional-decomposition-graph module's document. Its Requirement 2.3 is a
# byte-identical round trip, and its fixtures include the `crlf-line-endings.fdg` /
# `lf-line-endings.fdg` pair that collapses into one file under conversion - the exact pair the
# comment above exists to protect. Added BEFORE the first fixture was written, because a fixture
# committed under `text=auto` has already been normalised and the pair is already lost.
*.fdg   -text
# `.ghg` is the gartner-hypecycle-graph module's document, byte-compared for the same reason as
# `.fdg` (its Requirement 2.5), with the same `crlf-line-endings` / `lf-line-endings` fixture pair.
# Added before its first fixture, for the reason given above.
*.ghg   -text
# `.aad` is the agent-activity-diagram module's document. Its Requirement 2.7 is that an edit
# changes only the bytes it concerns, its fixtures' offsets are byte offsets, and agents write it
# with whatever line ending they have - so its bytes are the test subject, as `.fdg`'s are.
# Added before its first fixture, for the reason given above.
*.aad   -text
# `.ttl` and `.nt` belong to the RDF family, whose round-trip requirement byte-compares them -
# and unlike `.yml`, these extensions mean Turtle and N-Triples everywhere in the tree, so the
# by-extension rule applies exactly as written above: the guarantee follows the format.
*.ttl   -text
*.nt    -text
# `.rq` is the sparql-diagram module's query extension. The module never writes a query - its
# round-trip guarantee is held by having no writer at all - but its parser fixtures include a
# `crlf-line-endings.rq` / `lf-line-endings.rq` pair and a `no-trailing-newline.rq`, which are
# byte-compared and collapse into one file under conversion, exactly the pair the comment above
# exists to protect. And like `.ttl`, `.rq` means SPARQL everywhere in the tree.
*.rq    -text
# `.supply` is the supply-chain module's document, byte-compared for the same reason as `.fdg`:
# every write is a splice, an unchanged document round-trips byte-identical, and its fixtures
# include the `crlf-line-endings` / `lf-line-endings` pair. Added before its first fixture.
*.supply -text
# `.skv` is the sankey module's document, byte-compared for the same reason as `.supply`: every
# write is a splice, an unchanged document round-trips byte-identical, and its fixtures include the
# `crlf-line-endings` / `lf-line-endings` pair. Added before its first fixture.
*.skv   -text
# `.dis` is a DISL diagram specification, bundled verbatim from etalii-adp/etalii.adp into
# `src/diagrams/<module>/definition/` by `src/diagrams/tools/bundle-disl.sh`. Its bytes are the
# test subject in a different way from the documents above: `provenance.json` beside it records
# the sha256 of the bundled bytes, and each module's test recomputes that hash from the embedded
# resource. Under `eol=crlf` the working file - and so the embedded resource - would be CRLF while
# the upstream file is LF, and the hash would measure git rather than the definition. Added before
# the first `.dis` was committed, for the reason given above.
*.dis   -text
# A type's own FBL binding is bundled beside its `.dis` for the same reason and held by the same
# hash in the same provenance file. By folder, not by extension: every other `.fbl` in the tree
# is a module's own source or part of a corpus with rules of its own.
src/diagrams/*/definition/*.fbl -text

# `.yml` is deliberately NOT marked repo-wide, however byte-sensitive the Azure Pipelines module's
# fixtures are: it is far too common an extension, and a blanket rule would catch every workflow,
# compose file and configuration document in the tree. Scoped to the one folder where the bytes are
# the subject rather than the content.
src/diagrams/azure-devops-pipeline/backend/EtAlii.Adp.Diagram.AzureDevOpsPipeline.Tests/Fixtures/** -text

# The shared gate's fixtures are real runner logs, and the CRLF dotnet writes is part of what the
# gate's verdict is tested against: a log normalised to LF on commit would test a shape no real
# run produces. The folder holds only such logs, so a folder rule is exact here.
.github/tools/gate/fixtures/** -text

# The databricks module's fixtures, scoped the same way and for the same reason - with one turn
# tighter. What the by-extension rule above wants is for the guarantee to follow the format: a
# byte-compared document type earns one `-text` line naming its extension. The databricks module
# cannot have that, because its documents ARE Databricks' own `databricks.yml`, job resource YAML
# and pipeline settings JSON - `.yml` and `.json` are shared by every workflow, compose file and
# manifest in the tree, so marking them repo-wide would freeze line endings for files where the
# house style should simply apply. Hence a glob, like azure-devops-pipeline's - but naming the
# byte-compared extensions individually rather than the folder wholesale. That narrowing is what
# makes this glob safe where the withdrawn directory rule was not: `Fixtures/**` alone also
# catches the readme that documents the corpus (and any recorded verdicts or exports that join it
# later), which are ordinary text - the exact trap that got the directory rule withdrawn. These
# lines cannot catch them by construction, because they name only the extensions whose bytes are
# the test subject.
src/diagrams/databricks/backend/EtAlii.Adp.Diagram.Databricks.Tests/Fixtures/**/*.yml -text
src/diagrams/databricks/backend/EtAlii.Adp.Diagram.Databricks.Tests/Fixtures/**/*.yaml -text
src/diagrams/databricks/backend/EtAlii.Adp.Diagram.Databricks.Tests/Fixtures/**/*.json -text

# The FBL conformance corpus, vendored unchanged from etalii-adp/etalii.adp `specifications/fbl/`
# (fbl-implementation Requirement 10.3). Its fixture inputs and registrations are compared byte for
# byte - offsets in every fixture.json are UTF-8 byte offsets into them, and several are CRLF on
# purpose while others are LF - so git must not rewrite their endings. `.tml`, `.mm` and `.dsl` are
# already exempt by the extension rules above; these lines name the other byte-compared extensions,
# scoped to the vendored folder in the Databricks shape, so the readme beside them stays ordinary text.
src/backend/EtAlii.Adp.Specification.Fbl.Tests/Conformance/**/*.yml -text
src/backend/EtAlii.Adp.Specification.Fbl.Tests/Conformance/**/*.json -text
src/backend/EtAlii.Adp.Specification.Fbl.Tests/Conformance/**/*.cld -text
src/backend/EtAlii.Adp.Specification.Fbl.Tests/Conformance/**/*.adp -text

# `.owm` and the ansible-structure fixture tree are covered by `.gitattributes` files inside those
# modules rather than from here. Both are scoped more narrowly than an extension rule would be -
# `.owm` is byte-exact under the wardley-map tests and under `examples/example 2`, but not under
# `examples/example 1` - and both specs are still in flight. Left where their authors put them.
